NAME
    vstop - live htop-style dashboard of the .NET runtime this process runs on

SYNOPSIS
    vstop [-w]

DESCRIPTION
    The counterpart to 'coretop'. Where that one watches the emulated
    machine, this one watches the real one underneath it: the .NET virtual
    machine that DianaSh, the loaded core, and the frontend are all running
    on. Managed heap, garbage collector, thread pool, process memory and
    CPU, refreshing twice a second until you quit.

    It is the one dashboard that still has something to show with NO ROM
    loaded, because it reports on the host VM rather than a guest - it
    takes no IDebugTarget at all and is completely core-agnostic.

    Four bars across the top, colored green/yellow/red under 60% / 60-85% /
    over 85% the same way coretop's are:

        CPU        this process's processor time, divided by core count, so
                   100% means every core busy - htop's aggregate scale, not
                   the per-core one where an 8-core box can read 800%.
        Machine    system-wide memory in use. This is the figure the GC
                   compares against its own high-load threshold when it
                   decides to collect more aggressively, so it explains GC
                   behaviour that the heap numbers alone do not.
        Heap frag  fragmented bytes over committed bytes - dead space inside
                   the managed heap that the GC has not handed back. A high
                   reading next to a small live heap is the signature of a
                   pinning or large-object problem.
        Pool load  thread-pool threads against the pool maximum. Climbing
                   toward the ceiling with a non-zero queued-work count is
                   thread-pool starvation.

    Below them: process working set and private bytes; managed heap, GC-
    committed bytes, cumulative allocations and the live allocation rate;
    per-generation sizes (gen0/gen1/gen2, then LOH and POH where the
    runtime reports them); GC mode, latency mode, pause-time percentage,
    per-generation collection counts with a collections-per-minute rate and
    total time paused; OS thread and handle counts, pool thread counts and
    limits, queued and completed work items with a throughput rate; and the
    number of loaded assemblies.

    Allocation rate, CPU percent, collections per minute and work-item
    throughput are all rates, so they need two readings to exist. The first
    reading is taken before the first frame is drawn - you never see a
    screen of zeroes - but they stay zero for a single refresh if the
    dashboard is somehow drawn without that priming sample.

    A subtler point, and the reason several fields can read '-' instead of
    a number: GC.GetGCMemoryInfo() does not describe the heap right now, it
    describes the heap AS OF THE LAST COLLECTION. In a process that has not
    collected yet - which a freshly launched shell has not - every figure
    derived from it is legitimately zero: committed bytes, fragmented bytes,
    machine memory load, per-generation sizes, pause-time percentage. Those
    are exactly the readings someone opening this dashboard is most likely
    to act on, and a bar sitting confidently at 0.0% is worse than no bar,
    because it looks like an answer. So the Machine and Heap frag bars draw
    empty with '(no GC yet - press G)' until a collection has actually
    happened, and the committed/per-generation/pause-time fields show '-'.
    Press G once and the whole screen fills in. Working set, private bytes,
    managed heap, cumulative allocations, thread counts and CPU are all
    live readings and never do this.

    Nothing this command reads is allowed to throw. Every counter is read
    behind a guard that falls back to zero, because a runtime or platform
    that does not expose one of them (handle counts are not meaningful
    everywhere) must degrade to a missing number, not take the shell down.

    KEYS
        Ctrl+C, Q   exit. As in coretop, Ctrl+C is read as a key here (via
                    Console.TreatControlCAsInput) rather than raising a
                    process-level interrupt, and is restored the moment
                    vstop exits.
        G           force a full blocking GC.Collect(). Deliberately
                    included: the most common question this dashboard gets
                    opened to answer is 'is that a leak or just uncollected
                    garbage', and collecting on demand answers it in one
                    keystroke. It is a diagnostic, not something to lean on.

    Needs a REAL interactive terminal, same as 'nano' and 'coretop' and for
    the same reason - refuses cleanly rather than drawing anywhere when
    stdin/stdout is redirected or there is no terminal at all (a piped
    script, a 'source'd file, a headless test).

    In EmuSen.Mistress's own console window (a TextBox, not a real
    terminal - the above wouldn't work there at all), 'vstop' is replaced
    with a windowed equivalent, exactly as 'coretop' is: a real, non-
    blocking Avalonia window showing the same figures as widgets, with the
    four meters as real progress bars on the same green/yellow/red
    thresholds, refreshing on its own timer while gameplay keeps running.
    Ctrl+C, Q and G don't apply there - close the window to dismiss it, and
    use its 'Collect now' button for what G does here. The same window is
    on the menu bar at Settings > Runtime Dashboard..., which unlike
    Hardware Dashboard beside it is never greyed out, because this one has
    something to show with no ROM loaded.

    -w  Open the same dashboard in a separate window instead of taking over
        the terminal. Wired per frontend; where nothing is wired up it fails
        cleanly with "not supported by this frontend" rather than falling
        back to the terminal dashboard. Unlike coretop's -w, this one passes
        no debug target, because there is nothing here that depends on a
        loaded core. In EmuSen.Mistress vstop is already windowed, so -w is
        accepted and simply has no additional effect.

EXAMPLES
    vstop
    vstop -w
